// parity-expected-stdout-begin
// 1
// "s"
// parity-expected-stdout-end

// A lambda annotation naming the enclosing signature's type parameter.
//
// Filed as KI-070: the `a` in the lambda was said to become a nominal type
// `Adt("a")` rather than `outer`'s rigid variable, because
// `infer_lambda_expression` passed an explicitly empty `type_params` map.
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-11: this does not reproduce. If the annotation resolved to
// a nominal type, `f(x)` would be a mismatch and `outer` could not be used at
// two instantiations — both lines below print. The file is here so that a
// regression shows up as a snapshot diff.
//
// The entry's own repro is written `\y: a -> y`, which is a parse error for an
// unrelated reason — lambda parameter annotations require parentheses. That
// may be why it read as unfixed.

fn outer<a>(x: a) -> a {
    let f = \(y: a) -> y
    f(x)
}

fn main() with IO {
    print(outer(1))
    print(outer("s"))
}
